# __SERVER_NAME__

Generated from the canonical `ai-skills` Python MCP server template. It is an architecture seed using the official `mcp` 2.x SDK, one application-owned invocation path, and conservative local defaults.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This generated project is not production-accepted by generation alone. Replace sample domain behavior and complete the applicable security, CI/CD, deployment-artifact, and adoption work before production use.

## Quick start

### Local stdio

Stdio is the default transport and does not expose a network listener.

```bash
python -m pip install --require-hashes -r locks/dev-linux-x64-py312.lock
python -m pip install -e . --no-deps
__DISTRIBUTION__
```

A host should launch the installed entry point rather than `python src/...` so tests exercise the packaged application.

### Loopback Streamable HTTP

HTTP mode binds only to loopback and requires a development bearer token with at least 32 visible ASCII bytes.

```bash
export MCP_TRANSPORT=streamable-http
export MCP_HTTP_BEARER_TOKEN='replace-with-at-least-32-visible-ascii-bytes'
__DISTRIBUTION__
```

The MCP endpoint is `http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp`. Liveness and readiness are exposed at `/health/live` and `/health/ready` and contain no private data.

## Capabilities

The application-owned capability manifests in `src/__PACKAGE__/capabilities/` are the canonical static definitions, and runtime MCP discovery is the authoritative active view. The generated seed includes representative read, write, and capability-description operations so authorization and contract behavior can be exercised without maintaining a duplicate tool catalog in this README.

## Security

- Local stdio derives an explicit principal from the operating-system process boundary.
- Streamable HTTP binds only to loopback and requires `MCP_HTTP_BEARER_TOKEN` with at least 32 visible ASCII bytes.
- Remote deployment is not production-ready until the development bearer is replaced by a real identity provider and resource authorization.
- Writes are disabled unless `MCP_ENABLE_WRITES=1` and a server-verifiable, one-use HMAC approval record binds principal, capability, target, normalized arguments, expiry, and nonce.
- `put_item` uses optimistic concurrency: `expected_version=0` creates an absent item, successful writes return the next version, listings expose the current version, and updates must provide that version. The approval arguments digest includes `expected_version`, so a stale precondition cannot be detached from its approval.
- Capability manifests are validated against the copied canonical schema at import time.

## Development and verification

Run the repository-owned quality and exact-artifact path:

```bash
python -m pytest --junitxml=junit.xml
python scripts/assert_junit.py junit.xml --minimum-tests 1 --maximum-skips 0
python -m build --wheel
python scripts/smoke_artifact.py --distribution __DISTRIBUTION__ --package __PACKAGE__
```

The artifact smoke runs outside the checkout, verifies import provenance from `site-packages`, exercises supported protocol paths through the official MCP client, and checks a safe success and application failure path.

## Container

Build only after the wheel has passed artifact smoke:

```bash
WHEEL="$(find dist -maxdepth 1 -name '*.whl' -print -quit)"
docker build --build-arg "WHEEL=$WHEEL" --tag __DISTRIBUTION__:test .
docker run --rm --read-only --tmpfs /tmp:rw,noexec,nosuid,size=16m \
  --mount type=volume,source=__DISTRIBUTION__-state,target=/var/lib/__DISTRIBUTION__ \
  __DISTRIBUTION__:test
```

The Dockerfile installs the exact tested wheel and never rebuilds the package from source. For multi-architecture publication, build each platform once, test every child digest, compose an OCI index from those exact digests, and promote the unchanged index digest.
